# Out of Control - Kevin Kelly

Kevin Kelly's [[Out of Control - Kevin Kelly]] explores how intelligence and order emerge from complex, decentralized systems. The central thesis is that the most powerful and adaptive systems are those that are not centrally controlled—intelligence arises from self-organization, feedback loops, and emergent behavior. This work is referenced in the [[Implications]] mental model and provides the emergent and systemic perspective on intelligence.

## Key Themes

- Emergent intelligence: complex systems produce intelligent behavior without central direction.
- Distributed networks outperform centralized hierarchies in adaptability.
- The "out of control" nature of living systems and digital networks represents a higher form of intelligence.
- [[obstacles]] and [[reality-mental-model]] connect to Kelly's insights about the limits of control.

## Connection to Intelligence Model

Kelly's work shows that intelligence is not limited to individual minds but can arise from collective and distributed processes. This connects to [[Intelligence as a Mental Model]] and themes of [[obstacles]] and [[reality-mental-model]].

